<?xml version="1.0"?>
<robot xmlns:xacro="http://wiki.ros.org/xacro">

  <!--
    An Intel RealSense D405 depth camera bolted beside the gripper, looking the
    same way the fingers point.

    The shape, the mass and the frames are Intel's own: `realsense2_description`
    ships the model, so this file includes it and adds only the mount and the
    Gazebo sensor that renders through it.

    The D405 is the short-range camera of the RealSense family, and that is why
    it is the one here. Its working range is about 70 mm to 500 mm, which is
    uncomfortably close for a mobile robot and exactly right for a camera that
    rides on a wrist and is stood 300 mm off a glass to measure it. The other
    common choice, the D435, starts at about 300 mm, so the closest look this
    task takes would be at the very edge of what it can do.

    Because it rides on the wrist, pointing the tool at something is the same
    as pointing the camera at it, and the arm can walk the camera around a
    glass to see whether it has a handle, which one side-on view cannot show.

    Two things about the real camera matter to the code that reads it.

    **Its depth and colour pictures come from the same place.** Intel's own
    description puts the colour sensor at no offset from the depth sensor on
    this model, unlike the D435, so one optical frame serves for both and
    glasses/perception.py can project a colour pixel and a depth pixel with the
    same arithmetic.

    **It sees much more than the camera this project used to assume.** The D405
    covers about 87 degrees across, where the invented camera covered 60. That
    changes how much table one picture holds and therefore how many places the
    arm has to stand to survey it. Nothing in the code needed changing for it,
    because the measurements read the focal length out of the camera's own
    `camera_info` message rather than assuming one.
  -->

  <xacro:include filename="$(find realsense2_description)/urdf/_d405.urdf.xacro"/>

  <xacro:macro name="wrist_camera" params="prefix parent">

    <!-- Across the fingers rather than along them, and at the back of the
         gripper's body rather than level with its middle. Across, because the
         fingers swing in the tool's x direction and reach about 74 mm out
         either way when the gripper is open, so a camera mounted that way
         would sit inside their swing; across them the widest thing is the
         gripper's own 75 mm body. At the back, because this camera cannot
         focus closer than about 70 mm and the closest look it takes is down
         the fingers at a glass they are already round. The reasoning, and the
         distances it comes out at, are in arm/dimensions.py beside
         CAMERA_MOUNT. Turned a quarter turn so the camera looks along the
         tool's z axis, which is the direction the fingers point. -->
    <!-- The optical frames are optional in Intel's description and are off by
         default, because on real hardware the camera driver publishes better
         ones from the calibration stored in the device. In simulation there is
         no device to read, so the nominal frames are what there is, and the
         measurements need one: glasses/perception.py projects pixels in it. -->
    <xacro:sensor_d405
      parent="${parent}"
      name="${prefix}wrist_camera"
      use_nominal_extrinsics="true">
      <origin xyz="0 0.050 0.010" rpy="0 ${-pi/2} ${-pi/2}"/>
    </xacro:sensor_d405>

    <gazebo reference="${prefix}wrist_camera_link">
      <sensor name="wrist_camera" type="rgbd_camera">
        <always_on>true</always_on>
        <update_rate>15</update_rate>
        <topic>wrist_camera</topic>
        <camera>
          <!-- 87 degrees across, which is what Intel publish for the D405. -->
          <horizontal_fov>1.518</horizontal_fov>
          <!-- One of the camera's own stream sizes, in its real 16:9 shape.
               The shape has to be right, because Gazebo works the vertical
               field of view out from it: at 4:3 this camera would appear to
               see 71 degrees up and down instead of the 56 it really sees.

               The size is the smallest real mode that still resolves the
               table finely enough. A wide lens spreads its pixels over more
               ground, so this camera needs more of them than the 60 degree
               one it replaced: at 424 x 240 a pixel covers 2.0 mm of table
               from the survey height, which is coarser than the millimetre
               the measurements are reported to. At 640 x 360 it covers
               1.33 mm, which is fine, and the frames are still small enough
               to cross the ROS bridge comfortably. -->
          <image>
            <width>640</width>
            <height>360</height>
            <format>R8G8B8</format>
          </image>
          <!-- The camera's real working range. Modelling it as a hard limit is
               stricter than the hardware, which returns noisy depth outside it
               rather than nothing, and that is deliberate: a limit the
               simulator enforces is one that cannot be forgotten. It is also
               what caps SURVEY_HEIGHT in arm/dimensions.py, because a survey
               flown higher than this would be reading depth the camera cannot
               stand behind. -->
          <clip>
            <near>0.07</near>
            <far>0.50</far>
          </clip>
          <optical_frame_id>${prefix}wrist_camera_depth_optical_frame</optical_frame_id>
        </camera>
      </sensor>
    </gazebo>

  </xacro:macro>
</robot>
